I’ll first inspect the specimen metadata, diffraction grid, and the frozen analysis package, then build and refine the simplest chemically plausible multiphase GSAS-II model. I’ll keep each accepted checkpoint replayable and verify the final exports against the saved project.

The chemistry points first to wolframite-type MnWO₄, but I’m treating that only as the initial hypothesis. I’m now checking the frozen reference-search interface and the exact replay/export requirements, while independently extracting observed peak families so residual WO₃ or manganese-oxide phases are not hidden by the dominant product.

Peak-family extraction already supports at least two chemically ordinary phases: the wolframite family and residual hausmannite Mn₃O₄ (notably the 18°, 36°, 44°, 59.8°, and 64.8° sequence). The crowded 23–24° region may also require residual WO₃, so I’m comparing all exact-system COD references before fixing the inventory.

Three coherent families are now accounted for by MnWO₄, Mn₃WO₆, and monoclinic WO₃; the strongest remaining candidate lines are all explained without Mn₃O₄. I’m moving to full-pattern Rietveld checkpoints with the measured Kα doublet, Poisson weights, the known 200 mm Bragg–Brentano geometry, and physically bounded zero/displacement terms.

The first complete three-phase branch is stable and positive: preliminary refined crystalline fractions are about 67% MnWO₄, 12% Mn₃WO₆, and 20% WO₃. Peak positions improve coherently when all three cells are allowed modest shifts; I’m continuing through profile and coordinate checkpoints, with geometry screening before accepting any coordinate gain.

The unconstrained joint instrument-profile trial drove U and axial-divergence outside physical ranges, so I rejected that checkpoint and returned to the physical cell/calibration model. I’m now following the requested sequence strictly: heavy-site coordinates, oxygen coordinates with rollback screening, then phase size/strain and one instrument term at a time.

The coordinate checkpoints improve Rwp from 13.87% to 12.41% while retaining positive scales and only sub-percent cell changes. The all-coordinate WO₃ branch shows strong coordinate correlations, so I will accept it only if bond geometry remains sound; the current phase-profile refinement is still running.

Geometry screening rejects the oxygen-refined checkpoint: it creates sub-ångström O–O contacts. More importantly, the starting WO₃ reference in this branch already contains an implausibly short 1.58 Å O–O contact, so I will not submit it even if its profile is competitive. I’m screening the exact-composition WO₃ references now and will continue from the closest physically sound monoclinic topology.

The physically sound WO₃ reference gives a comparable cell-stage fit (Rwp 13.80%) and normal W–O/O–O geometry. GSAS-II’s direct crystallite-size derivative is numerically stalling even one phase at a time, so I’m treating that as unknown—not rejection—and switching to bounded one-dimensional profile searches followed by fixed-value refinements.

The fixed-value test confirms the numerical workaround is effective: setting only MnWO₄ size to 0.10 µm reduced Rwp from 13.80% to 11.18% without changing topology or geometry. A bounded coordinate-descent search is now evaluating the remaining phase widths and instrument terms; every candidate keeps positive phase scales and the atomic model fixed.

The three phase-size families all favor the physically allowed lower end of the lab range (about 0.05 µm), bringing Rwp to 7.48%. That broadening is consistent across MnWO₄, WO₃, and Mn₃WO₆ rather than being absorbed into an unphysical instrument width; strain and instrument grids are still in progress.

Strain contributes little compared with size: MnWO₄ stays near 1000 ppm, and WO₃ selects about 1800 ppm for only a 0.003 percentage-point Rwp gain. This is reassuring—the large improvement is from coherent crystallite broadening, not a high-strain artifact.

Mn₃WO₆ is the only phase needing substantial strain broadening, selecting the upper tested 8000 ppm limit and reducing Rwp to 7.28%; its weaker peaks make that value less precise. The instrument W term remains at the ordinary starting value of 10, so the fit is not trading phase strain against an extreme instrumental Gaussian width.

Instrument U settles at 0 and X near 1.2, both within the stated laboratory ranges; the fit is now Rwp 7.27%. I’m finishing axial-divergence/polarization checks and a second phase-width pass, then I’ll map any coherent residual families before deciding whether coordinate refinement is warranted.

Axial divergence stays at 0.02 and polarization selects 0.9, lowering Rwp to 7.10% with no boundary pathology. The second width pass is confirming the ~0.05 µm sizes rather than drifting them after polarization was set.

The second pass leaves MnWO₄ and WO₃ unchanged at 0.05 µm to numerical precision. I’m waiting on the minor Mn₃WO₆ width/strain pass; after that I’ll inspect the difference profile peak-by-peak and test only supported residual complexity.

A second bounded strain pass adjusts MnWO₄ modestly from 1000 to 1800 ppm and improves Rwp to 7.085%; this remains well inside the physical range. WO₃ and Mn₃WO₆ are the final profile terms being rechecked.

The bounded physical profile converged at Rwp 7.085% with crystalline fractions 63.2% MnWO₄, 17.0% Mn₃WO₆, and 19.8% WO₃. Because all three sizes selected the lowest coarse-grid point, I’m checking the exact 0.04 µm lower bound before residual interpretation; then I’ll avoid any further search once coherent families are accounted for.

The residual map shows derivative/intensity pairs at already assigned reflections—not a new peak family. The strongest are around the MnWO₄ 29.8/30.2° pair and the WO₃ 22.9–24.2° triplet, so I’m re-optimizing cells and common angular calibration with the accepted physical widths fixed before touching coordinates.

After cell/calibration re-optimization, Rwp is 6.876% and all residual maxima still sit on accepted-phase reflections. The dominant 29.8/30.2° MnWO₄ intensity ratio is the clearest structural mismatch, so I’m testing MnWO₄ coordinates phase-by-phase with short steps and explicit bond-length rollback—not adding another phase.

MnWO₄ heavy-site refinement is physical and small (~0.02 Å), improving Rwp to 6.735%. A free oxygen step improves further but moves sites by ~0.17–0.27 Å—far beyond the documented reference-mismatch range—so I’m rejecting that free endpoint and testing only interpolated oxygen shifts capped near 0.05 Å RMS.

The bounded MnWO₄ oxygen shift at 0.046 Å RMS is accepted (Rwp 6.559%); larger shifts continue improving the fit but exceed the documented mismatch range. WO₃’s free heavy-site step similarly overshoots (~0.06–0.10 Å), so I’m applying the same bounded interpolation rule before considering oxygen sites.

WO₃ bounded heavy and oxygen shifts are now capped at 0.047 Å and 0.048 Å RMS, respectively; the free oxygen endpoint was rejected. The accepted bounded WO₃ model reaches Rwp 6.410% with normal starting topology preserved. I’m applying the same final check to Mn₃WO₆.

Mn₃WO₆ coordinate checks converge to bounded shifts of 0.046 Å (Mn) and 0.047 Å RMS (O); the accepted three-phase model is now Rwp 6.372%. I’m screening all final bond networks and the remaining residual peaks before the last specimen-effect tests and export.

The phase inventory is now chemically and crystallographically complete: MnWO₄, Mn₃WO₆, and residual WO₃ account for every coherent reflection family. I’m finishing only bounded specimen-effect checks, then I’ll freeze and independently replay the physical GSAS-II model before writing the deliverables.

Surface roughness was decisively unsupported—the uncorrected model remained best, while every nonzero correction worsened the fit. I’m now testing March–Dollase texture phase-by-phase on crystallographically distinct axes, with fixed bounded coefficients and re-equilibrated scales/background; only a reproducible, material improvement will be retained.

The dominant MnWO₄ phase is already strongly favoring a random orientation: coarse texture coefficients on the first tested axis worsen Rwp from 6.37% to 9–16%. I’m letting the remaining independent axes finish so this conclusion is based on full directional coverage.

The complete directional screen found one clear, physically modest texture term: MnWO₄ [111], with March–Dollase \(r\approx1.18\), lowers Rwp substantially from 6.37% to 5.66%. All other MnWO₄ axes were worse. I’ll tighten that coefficient locally, then test whether either minor phase has independent texture support.

Mn₃WO₆ shows, at most, a very shallow texture response so far (about 0.02 percentage points in Rwp), far smaller than the MnWO₄ effect. I’ll retain it only if the full screen gives a distinct minimum and the improvement survives local refinement; otherwise the minor phase remains random.

WO₃ has a second unmistakable texture signature: [010] with \(r=1.18\) drops Rwp from 5.61% to 3.68%, whereas nearby coarse alternatives on other axes are much worse. This is broad intensity redistribution, not a peak-position patch; I’m completing the axis screen and will then locate the bounded minimum.

The WO₃ texture minimum is well bounded at [010], \(r\approx1.30\): Rwp rises on both sides (3.44% at 1.25, 3.47% at 1.35, and >4% beyond 1.6). I’m now allowing only the three already-supported texture coefficients to settle continuously from their bounded minima, with axes and all structural/profile terms fixed.

Re-equilibrating lattice metrics after the accepted texture corrections improved Rwp further to 3.30% with only small, plausible cell shifts (≤0.05% in lengths). I’m giving that same cell-only stage one convergence pass, then will inspect residual families and test no additional structural complexity unless a specific defect remains.

Residual extrema are centered on accepted-phase peaks and have center/shoulder sign reversals, indicating width mismatch rather than another phase. Because the original crystallite-size grid was chosen before texture was modeled, I’m re-screening only isotropic sizes on the completed three-phase state; this is the next supported complexity and keeps all axes, coordinates, cells, and texture fixed.

The completed model confirms all three crystallite sizes at their previous bounded values (0.050, 0.040, and 0.050 μm); moving any one by even 0.02 μm worsens Rwp sharply. I’m now doing the analogous one-at-a-time microstrain check, especially because Mn₃WO₆ sits at the stated 8000 ppm upper bound.

Microstrain is also bounded cleanly: MnWO₄ remains 1800 ppm, WO₃ shifts to a shallow but stable 1000 ppm minimum, and Mn₃WO₆ genuinely prefers the allowed 8000 ppm ceiling. I’m making one final local instrument-width pass; this checks whether the residual center/shoulder pattern belongs to the shared optics rather than any phase.

The shared optics now have bounded interior minima (approximately \(W=4\), \(U=5\), \(X=1.8\), SH/L=0.02), lowering Rwp to 3.20%. I’m allowing only \(U,W,X\) to settle continuously from those screened values, then I’ll lock every model parameter and perform the final replay/geometry checks.

The unconstrained optics trial was rejected: it lowered Rwp numerically but drove both Gaussian \(U\) and \(W\) negative, making the profile width nonphysical across the scan. The accepted checkpoint remains the bounded positive model at Rwp 3.196%; this is exactly the sort of small numerical gain that does not justify bad geometry/profile physics.

Joint zero/displacement refinement improves Rwp to 3.155% but is 99.91% correlated and pushes displacement to 789 μm, almost the 800 μm physical limit. I’m checking zero-only and displacement-only alternatives from the same checkpoint; the least-correlated physical calibration will be used even if the joint fit is numerically a little lower.

The best physical checkpoint is now stable at Rwp 3.152%. All coherent peaks remain assigned, all scales are positive, lattice/coordinate geometry is ordinary, and further unconstrained width fitting was rejected. I’m freezing that checkpoint, exporting the CIF/profile/JSON set, and then running the independent GSAS-II replay validator before submission.

Independent replay succeeded without using cached calculated intensities: the regenerated profile matches `fit.csv` to \(5\times10^{-9}\) counts, all analytic peak widths/backgrounds are positive, all ADPs pass the tensor check, and the project remains unchanged. I’m completing the final bond-distance and file-schema audit, then writing the concise evidence/uncertainty report.

